using Global

~ flashback_start

# Setting: A year and a half ago. Warehouse floor / shipping office. Marcus, a logistics coordinator, has flagged the manifest.

Marcus: Jason, this manifest doesn't match the export license. End-user field is blank.

Marcus: I can't clear this for shipping without it.

Jason: It's a discretion account. Compliance already looked at it.

Marcus: I called compliance. They said they've never heard of this shipment.

- Tell him the truth, carefully. [if leverage >= 0] => marcus_told
- Pull rank. [if compliance >= 0] => marcus_pressured
- Offer him something to look away. => marcus_bought

~ marcus_told

Jason: Because it's not supposed to go through the normal channel, Marcus. That's the point.
do leverage += 1

Marcus: (long pause) You're telling me to knowingly ship this.

Marcus: I have a kid, Jason. I can't have my name on this if it goes wrong.

Jason: It won't go wrong. It never has.

Marcus: "Never" is doing a lot of work in that sentence.

do Global.marcus_path = "told"

=> shipment_pivot

~ marcus_pressured

Jason: I don't need to explain this to you. Clear it or I'll have someone else clear it.
do compliance += 1
do leverage -= 1

Marcus: (jaw tight) Fine. It's cleared. It's your signature on it, not mine.

Marcus: I want that in writing.

Jason: Whatever gets it moving.

do Global.marcus_path = "pressured"

=> shipment_pivot

~ marcus_bought

Jason: Look, if this is about the numbers on your end — I can make sure that's worth your while.
do leverage += 2
do compliance -= 1

Marcus: (quiet) ...How much are we talking.

Jason: Enough that this stops being a conversation you have to have again.

Marcus: (he doesn't answer. He clears the manifest.)

do Global.marcus_path = "bought"

=> shipment_pivot

~ shipment_pivot

Jason: (The truck left that night. Marcus stopped making eye contact with him after that.)

do Global.return_from_flashback()

=> END
